"OverallSummary": "George Apolonides (also known as George Apollo) violated Regulation Best Interest's Care Obligation by engaging in excessive trading in four customers' accounts between March 2016 and April 2022, three of whom were senior customers. Apolonides exercised de facto control over these accounts as customers relied on his advice and routinely followed his recommendations. The excessive trading is evidenced by: (1) high turnover rates ranging from 17 to 38 (well above the industry threshold of 6), (2) high cost-to-equity ratios ranging from 71% to 125% (well above the 20% threshold), and (3) significant financial harm to customers. The trading in these accounts generated $618,911 in total trading costs, including $563,263 in commissions, while causing $735,376 in total realized losses. For Customer A, a 69-year-old physician, Apolonides recommended 357 transactions resulting in a turnover rate of 31, a cost-to-equity ratio of 114%, $229,352 in trading costs (including $202,964 in commissions), and $326,684 in realized losses. For Customer B, a 79-year-old semi-retired banker, Apolonides recommended 127 transactions resulting in a turnover rate of 17, a cost-to-equity ratio of 76%, $158,322 in trading costs (including $148,864 in commissions), and $115,721 in realized losses. Both Customers A and B had 'speculation' as their investment objective. FINRA found that Apolonides willfully violated Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(1) (Regulation BI) and FINRA Rules 2111 and 2010 by recommending excessive trading that was not in his customers' best interests. As a result, Apolonides received an 11-month suspension from associating with any FINRA member in all capacities."
